@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_NavGroup}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Overview}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_NavGroup @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Overview</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_NavGroup @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Overview</h1>


<p>
    The Nav Group component is designed to be used within an HTML<span class="code-span">nav</span> element and uses what is known as a "disclosure" pattern to show or hide content, which is
    also a common pattern used in accessible component/widgets. In this instance the shown content just pushes the other content on the page out of the way as.opposed to a dropdown where a panel 
    why may obscure content. Given this behaviour its suitable for side navigation systems, such as the one on this site and was why it was built and then subsequently released.
</p>

<p>
    The Nav Group itself can contain <b>NavGroupLink</b> components, <b>NavSection</b> components (which provide the disclosure behaviour), or a <b>NavSeparator</b> (decorative line / separator).
    Each <b>NavSection</b> can in turn contain <b>NavGroupLink</b>, <b>NavSeparator</b> and <b>NavSection</b> components allowing you to build 'N'-level deep menu systems.
</p>

<p>
    Each <b>NavGroup</b> can be labelled by an external element and outputs an HTML <span class="code-span">ul</span> element, ready to accept its child components, which are all rendered as <span class="code-span">li</span> elements.
    The <b>NavSection</b> component contains a <span class="code-span">button</span> element and a <span class="code-span">ul</span> element, and it is the nesting of <b>NavSection</b> components that creates a series of disclosures.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>The NavGroup component is designed to be used on an interactive page.
</p>
<p>
    The code below shows the "Frameworks" section of the left side navigaion on this site, which is compried of a <span class="code-span">h2</span> element connect to a <b>NavGrooup</b>  component that contains a <b>NavSeparator</b> followed by a <b>NavSection</b> containing <b>NavGroupLink</b> components.
</p>

<CodeBox CodeBlock="@NavGroupSnippets.Framework_Section" AriaLabel="Nav group code example" />

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>You may be wondering what the <span class="code-span">VisuallyHiddenPrefix</span> Parameter is for. To give more context to screen reader users, when the link title is read out to them, 
    they will hear announcements such as "Modal Dialog Overview" in stead of just overview as each section has the same page names. Another reason for why I do this is that screen reader users also have
    access to lists (of shortcuts) to items on a page such as links, and without this prefix in that list would be serveral links all just called overview, which is not helpful. 
</p>

<p>
    The NavGroup has been coded so that if the current page matches a link within a closed NavSection, that all sections will open so the link is visible.
</p>

<div class="flex-content justify-content-between">
